WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.320
Omar looks after
a small apartment building.

2
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.410
Tonight the courtyard is cold,

3
00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.736
and the flat upstairs needs warming.

4
00:00:07.736 --> 00:00:11.486
The heat pump’s outdoor unit sits right there
in that cold courtyard.

5
00:00:11.486 --> 00:00:13.605
So where does the warmth come from?

6
00:00:13.795 --> 00:00:15.785
Cold air isn’t empty of heat.

7
00:00:15.785 --> 00:00:20.196
A heat pump uses electricity
to move some of that heat indoors.

8
00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:21.506
Meet our heat courier.

9
00:00:21.506 --> 00:00:22.876
It isn’t a real part.

10
00:00:22.876 --> 00:00:25.456
It stands for energy, so we can follow it.

11
00:00:25.546 --> 00:00:26.986
Follow the sealed loop.

12
00:00:26.986 --> 00:00:30.716
Outside, very cold refrigerant
flows through a coil.

13
00:00:30.716 --> 00:00:33.056
It’s even colder than the courtyard air,

14
00:00:33.056 --> 00:00:35.991
so it absorbs heat and turns into a gas.

15
00:00:36.081 --> 00:00:37.751
Next comes the compressor.

16
00:00:37.751 --> 00:00:40.941
It runs on electricity, and it squeezes the gas,

17
00:00:40.941 --> 00:00:43.457
raising its pressure and its temperature.

18
00:00:43.547 --> 00:00:46.147
Now the refrigerant is hotter than the room.

19
00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:48.697
In the indoor coil, it gives up its heat

20
00:00:48.697 --> 00:00:50.597
and turns back into a liquid.

21
00:00:50.687 --> 00:00:53.117
Then an expansion valve drops its pressure,

22
00:00:53.117 --> 00:00:54.872
and its temperature falls,

23
00:00:54.872 --> 00:00:56.797
so it can collect heat outside again.

24
00:00:56.797 --> 00:00:58.497
Round and round.

25
00:00:58.587 --> 00:01:00.767
The refrigerant stays inside the loop.

26
00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:03.724
No outdoor air is being piped into the room.

27
00:01:03.914 --> 00:01:06.674
Here’s a made-up example, not a measurement.

28
00:01:06.674 --> 00:01:09.034
Three units of heat collected outside,

29
00:01:09.034 --> 00:01:10.964
plus one unit of electricity,

30
00:01:10.964 --> 00:01:13.504
deliver four units of heat inside.

31
00:01:13.594 --> 00:01:15.094
That’s not free energy.

32
00:01:15.094 --> 00:01:17.374
Three of the four came from the courtyard.

33
00:01:17.374 --> 00:01:22.401
The electricity is the effort
that moves heat uphill, from cold to warm.

34
00:01:22.591 --> 00:01:23.950
Real results vary.

35
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.520
The colder it is outside, the bigger the lift,

36
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.041
and the harder the compressor has to work.

37
00:01:29.041 --> 00:01:32.928
The system, its installation
and the building matter too.

38
00:01:33.018 --> 00:01:35.478
Many heat pumps can also run in reverse,

39
00:01:35.478 --> 00:01:37.008
to cool a room in summer.

40
00:01:37.008 --> 00:01:38.614
Not all of them do.

41
00:01:38.804 --> 00:01:41.574
So if you’re weighing one up,
ask three questions.

42
00:01:41.574 --> 00:01:43.134
Where will it collect heat?

43
00:01:43.134 --> 00:01:44.974
How big a lift must it make?

44
00:01:44.974 --> 00:01:47.271
And is it designed for this building?

45
00:01:47.561 --> 00:01:49.861
A heat pump doesn’t make most of its heat.

46
00:01:49.861 --> 00:01:53.151
It moves it,
and it needs electricity to do that.
